1. Start / Stop / Restart Jenkins

Linux (systemd):

sudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl stop jenkins
sudo systemctl restart jenkins
sudo systemctl status jenkins

Enable Jenkins at boot:

sudo systemctl enable jenkins
2. Check Jenkins logs
sudo journalctl -u jenkins

Live logs:

sudo journalctl -u jenkins -f

Last 100 lines:

sudo journalctl -u jenkins -n 100
3. Check Jenkins service
systemctl is-active jenkins
systemctl is-enabled jenkins
4. Jenkins port

Jenkins normally runs on port 8080.

Check whether port 8080 is being used:

sudo ss -tulpn | grep 8080

Or:

sudo lsof -i :8080
5. Jenkins home directory

Usually:

/var/lib/jenkins

Check:

ls -la /var/lib/jenkins

Check disk space:

df -h

Check Jenkins directory size:

sudo du -sh /var/lib/jenkins

This is especially useful for the "No space left on device" issue you were troubleshooting recently.

6. Jenkins CLI

If Jenkins CLI is configured:

java -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s http://localhost:8080/

List jobs:

java -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s http://localhost:8080/ list-jobs

Build a job:

java -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s http://localhost:8080/ build JOB_NAME
7. Jenkins process

Find Jenkins process:

ps aux | grep jenkins

Find Java processes:

ps -ef | grep java
8. Useful Linux commands for Jenkins troubleshooting
free -h
df -h
du -sh /var/lib/jenkins/*
top
htop
uptime
